.TH ODE 2
.SH NAME
ode: init, new, del, addmass, addspring, remmass, remspring, clearforces, update, pause, renum, massfind, d3, d3add, d3sub, d3mul, d3dot, d3norm, d3normsqr, d3dist, d3unit \- mass-spring ordinary differential equation solver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "ode.m";
ode := load Ode Ode->PATH;
ODE, Mass, Spring, D3: import ode;

MSF_INACTIVE, MSF_FIXED: con 1 << iota;
SSF_INACTIVE, SSF_NO_COMPRESSION, SSF_NO_TENSION: con 1 << iota;

D3: adt { x, y, z: real; };

Mass: adt {
	x, y, z:   real;
	vx, vy, vz: real;
	fx, fy, fz: real;
	mass:      real;
	drag:      real;
	flags:     int;
	num:       int;
	radius:    real;
	userdata:  int;
};

Spring: adt {
	end1, end2:    ref Mass;
	const:         real;
	restlen:       real;
	f:             real;
	displacement:  real;
	flags:         int;
	num:           int;
	userdata:      int;
};

ODE: adt {
	masses:  list of ref Mass;
	springs: list of ref Spring;
	drag_v, drag_v2, drag_v3: real;
	accel_limit: real;
	t:       real;
	t_scale: real;
	paused:  int;
	h:       real;
};

init:        fn();
new:         fn(): ref ODE;
del:         fn(ode: ref ODE);
addmass:     fn(ode: ref ODE, m: ref Mass);
addspring:   fn(ode: ref ODE, s: ref Spring);
remmass:     fn(ode: ref ODE, m: ref Mass);
remspring:   fn(ode: ref ODE, s: ref Spring);
clearforces: fn(ode: ref ODE);
update:      fn(ode: ref ODE, dt: real);
pause:       fn(ode: ref ODE, on: int);
renum:       fn(ode: ref ODE);
massfind:    fn(ode: ref ODE, x, y, z: real): ref Mass;

d3:        fn(x, y, z: real): D3;
d3add:     fn(a, b: D3): D3;
d3sub:     fn(a, b: D3): D3;
d3mul:     fn(s: real, a: D3): D3;
d3dot:     fn(a, b: D3): real;
d3norm:    fn(a: D3): real;
d3normsqr: fn(a: D3): real;
d3dist:    fn(a, b: D3): real;
d3unit:    fn(a: D3): D3;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ode
.RB ( /dis/lib/ode.dis )
is a compact explicit integrator for systems of point masses connected by
Hookean springs.
Programs under
.IR temple (1)
use it for interactive physics.
.PP
.B Init
prepares the module.
.B New
allocates an empty
.B ODE
world;
.B del
frees it.
Masses and springs are linked into the world with
.B addmass
and
.BR addspring ,
and removed with
.B remmass
and
.BR remspring .
.B Renum
renumbers the
.B num
fields after structural edits.
.B Massfind
returns a mass near
.IR (x,y,z) ,
or
.B nil .
.PP
Each
.B Mass
stores position, velocity, and a force accumulator
.BR (fx,fy,fz) .
.B MSF_FIXED
holds a mass in place;
.B MSF_INACTIVE
excludes it from integration.
.B Drag
is a per-mass factor combined with the world's
.BR drag_v ,
.BR drag_v2 ,
and
.B drag_v3
terms.
.B Radius
is not used by the integrator itself; games store a collision radius there.
.PP
Each
.B Spring
joins
.B end1
and
.BR end2 ,
with Hooke constant
.B const
and rest length
.BR restlen .
.B SSF_NO_COMPRESSION
and
.B SSF_NO_TENSION
restrict the force to one side of the rest length;
.B SSF_INACTIVE
disables the spring.
After
.BR update ,
.B f
and
.B displacement
hold the last computed force magnitude and length error.
.PP
The recommended force contract is:
call
.B clearforces
to zero every mass accumulator, add application forces into
.BR fx/fy/fz ,
then call
.BI update( ode , dt )
with a wall-clock step in seconds.
.B Update
scales
.I dt
by
.BR t_scale ,
runs the spring and drag pipeline, converts force to acceleration, optionally
clips by
.BR accel_limit ,
and advances positions with an RK4 step of size related to
.BR h .
.B Pause
freezes integration when
.I on
is non-zero.
.PP
The
.B d3*
helpers manipulate
.B D3
triples for game code that prefers value vectors over mutating masses
directly.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
	ode->init();
	world := ode->new();
	world.t_scale = 1.0;
	m0 := ref Mass;
	m0.x = 0.0; m0.y = 1.0; m0.z = 0.0;
	m0.mass = 1.0;
	ode->addmass(world, m0);
	# ... add more masses and springs ...
	ode->clearforces(world);
	m0.fy -= 9.8 * m0.mass;   # gravity
	ode->update(world, 0.016);
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/ode.b
.br
.B /module/ode.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR temple (1),
.IR rocket (1),
.IR span (1),
.IR strut (1),
.IR math-fp (2)
.SH BUGS
Collision detection is left to the application. Stiff springs or large steps can diverge.
